<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE mapper PUBLIC "-//mybatis.org//DTD Mapper 3.0//EN" "https://mybatis.org/dtd/mybatis-3-mapper.dtd">

<!--
  The transaction-limit engine. Six of these statements read and write the legacy limit
  chain (CORP_LMT_PC_DTL, CORP_USR_GRP_LMT, COM_LMT_PC_DTL and the COM_ST_SRVC_CCY_MTRX /
  COM_ST_SRVC_CCY matrix all three hang off); one reads this service's own TRX_TASK (V1,
  widened by V13). This service creates none of the legacy tables and Flyway must never
  try - but it does WRITE two of them, which is not a contradiction: the legacy DIRECT
  schema is the live schema of the system this application replaces, so the no-DDL rule and
  the write path stand side by side.

  Legacy columns are NVARCHAR2, so every literal compared against one is written N'...' - a
  plain 'N' next to an NVARCHAR2 column raises ORA-12704 (character set mismatch). TRX_TASK
  follows the same style, so the rule holds throughout this file, including the status list
  in sumOpenTaskUsage.

  A LIMIT IS PER CURRENCY COMBINATION, NOT PER CURRENCY. This is the single fact that most
  of this file exists to get right. COM_ST_SRVC_CCY carries the SRVC_CD; COM_ST_SRVC_CCY_MTRX
  hangs off it and carries CCY_MTRX_CD, whose values are LL (local to local), LF (local to
  forex), FL (forex to local), FS (forex to forex, same currency) and FC (forex to forex,
  cross). Those are CATEGORY PAIRS, not currency codes. A limit row then carries its own
  CCY_CD, and that is a different thing again: the DENOMINATION the ceiling is stated in.
  So the matrix cell selects WHICH ceiling applies and CCY_CD says what units it is in. An
  amount has to be converted into CCY_CD before it is compared or added; skip that and a
  100 USD transfer sails past an IDR ceiling every single time.

  TWO CEILINGS PER ROW. MAX_AMT_LMT / AMT_LMT_USAGE are money in the row's CCY_CD;
  MAX_OC_LMT / OC_LMT_USAGE are a transaction COUNT with no currency whatsoever. Both are
  nullable and NULL is what means "no ceiling on this dimension". ZERO MEANS BLOCKED - the
  permissive reading of a zero ceiling is the one mistake in this area that lets money out
  of the bank.

  CORP_USR_GRP_LMT HAS NO OCCURRENCE COLUMNS. Verified against the live schema on
  2026-09-07: the table is ID, VERSION, MAX_AMT_LMT, AMT_LMT_USAGE, SRVC_CD, the four audit
  columns, CCY_CD, SRVC_CCY_MTRX_ID, CORP_USR_GRP_ID - and that is all. Legacy simply never
  gave the group ceiling a transaction count. lockGroupLimit therefore selects typed NULLs
  into the two occurrence fields of LimitRow rather than pretending the columns exist, and
  addGroupUsage takes the count parameter (so both ladders can be driven with one shape)
  without writing it anywhere. Adding those columns is NOT an option: they would be DDL
  against a legacy table.

  THE PER-COMPANY ROW IS OFTEN MISSING AND MUST BE CREATED. 1,712 of 2,303 active companies
  have no CORP_LMT_PC_DTL row for lines their package does define, because the legacy
  procedure INJECT_LIMIT_COMPANY was never run for them. A company with no row of its own
  has nowhere to record a reservation - the package is a template shared by everyone on it
  and has no usage columns - so insertCorpLimitFromPackage materializes the row from the
  package before the first reservation. That is the same copy-down the legacy procedure
  performs, done at transaction time instead of at company setup.

  DUPLICATES EXIST AND ROWNUM = 1 TOLERATES THEM. Neither limit table has a unique key
  beyond ID (direct-bankmodule's LimitCatalogMapper documents 77 duplicated corp pairs), so
  the resolution can match more than one row. Both lock statements take the first and carry
  its ID out, because re-running the resolution for the update could land on a DIFFERENT
  duplicate than the one that was locked - which would increment a row nobody holds a lock
  on. ROWNUM rather than FETCH FIRST because Oracle refuses FOR UPDATE over a row-limiting
  clause, and FOR UPDATE OF <alias>.<column> rather than a bare FOR UPDATE because with the
  two matrix tables joined in, a bare FOR UPDATE would try to lock the master rows too.
-->
<mapper namespace="id.co.bni.direct.transaction.repository.mapper.LimitMapper">

    <!--
      The matrix leg all four limit reads share. COM_ST_SRVC_CCY is where SRVC_CD lives;
      COM_ST_SRVC_CCY_MTRX is where CCY_MTRX_CD lives. Both are needed on every read
      precisely because neither table alone can answer "the LL ceiling for this service".
      Neither carries a soft-delete flag, so there is nothing further to filter on here.
    -->
    <sql id="ccyMatrix">
        JOIN COM_ST_SRVC_CCY_MTRX scm ON scm.ID = v.SRVC_CCY_MTRX_ID
        JOIN COM_ST_SRVC_CCY scy      ON scy.ID = scm.SRVC_CCY_ID
    </sql>

    <!--
      The company ceiling, locked. Selected columns are the whole LimitRow: the ID so the
      increment addresses exactly the row that was locked, SRVC_CCY_MTRX_ID and CCY_MTRX_CD
      so the caller can freeze onto the task what it validated against (V13), CCY_CD so it
      knows what currency to convert the amount into, and both ceiling/usage pairs.

      SRVC_CD is filtered on the matrix side (scy.SRVC_CD) rather than on v.SRVC_CD: the
      matrix is the authoritative link between service and cell, and the denormalized copy
      on the limit row is not guaranteed to agree with it.
    -->
    <select id="lockCorpLimit" resultType="id.co.bni.direct.transaction.entity.LimitRows$LimitRow">
        SELECT v.ID               AS "id",
               v.SRVC_CCY_MTRX_ID AS "srvcCcyMtrxId",
               scm.CCY_MTRX_CD    AS "ccyMtrxCd",
               v.CCY_CD           AS "ccyCd",
               v.MAX_AMT_LMT      AS "maxAmtLmt",
               v.AMT_LMT_USAGE    AS "amtLmtUsage",
               v.MAX_OC_LMT       AS "maxOcLmt",
               v.OC_LMT_USAGE     AS "ocLmtUsage"
        FROM CORP_LMT_PC_DTL v
        <include refid="ccyMatrix"/>
        WHERE v.CORP_ID = #{companyId}
          AND scy.SRVC_CD = #{srvcCd}
          AND scm.CCY_MTRX_CD = #{ccyMtrxCd}
          AND ROWNUM = 1
        FOR UPDATE OF v.AMT_LMT_USAGE
    </select>

    <!--
      The user-group ceiling, locked the same way. The two occurrence columns are selected
      as typed NULLs because CORP_USR_GRP_LMT does not have them (see the header): CAST
      rather than a bare NULL so Oracle reports a NUMBER and MyBatis maps it to the record's
      Long instead of guessing from an untyped literal.
    -->
    <select id="lockGroupLimit" resultType="id.co.bni.direct.transaction.entity.LimitRows$LimitRow">
        SELECT v.ID                 AS "id",
               v.SRVC_CCY_MTRX_ID   AS "srvcCcyMtrxId",
               scm.CCY_MTRX_CD      AS "ccyMtrxCd",
               v.CCY_CD             AS "ccyCd",
               v.MAX_AMT_LMT        AS "maxAmtLmt",
               v.AMT_LMT_USAGE      AS "amtLmtUsage",
               CAST(NULL AS NUMBER) AS "maxOcLmt",
               CAST(NULL AS NUMBER) AS "ocLmtUsage"
        FROM CORP_USR_GRP_LMT v
        <include refid="ccyMatrix"/>
        WHERE v.CORP_USR_GRP_ID = #{groupId}
          AND scy.SRVC_CD = #{srvcCd}
          AND scm.CCY_MTRX_CD = #{ccyMtrxCd}
          AND ROWNUM = 1
        FOR UPDATE OF v.AMT_LMT_USAGE
    </select>

    <!--
      The limit-package template, the fallback for the two thirds of companies with no row
      of their own. Entered from CORP because the package is not held on the limit row: the
      company names a SERVICE package (CORP.SRVC_PC_CD), the service package names a LIMIT
      package (COM_SRVC_PC.LIMIT_PACKAGE_FK, spelled unlike every other package FK on that
      table), and the limit package is what COM_LMT_PC_DTL hangs off by LMT_PC_CD.

      CORP.IS_DELETE = N'N' is checked here because CORP has the soft-delete flag the limit
      tables lack - it is the only place in this chain where a dead company can be caught.
      No FOR UPDATE and no lock: the package is a template nobody reserves against, and
      locking it would serialize every company that shares it.
    -->
    <select id="findPackageLimit" resultType="id.co.bni.direct.transaction.entity.LimitRows$PackageLimitRow">
        SELECT v.SRVC_CCY_MTRX_ID AS "srvcCcyMtrxId",
               scm.CCY_MTRX_CD    AS "ccyMtrxCd",
               v.CCY_CD           AS "ccyCd",
               v.MAX_AMT_LMT      AS "maxAmtLmt",
               v.MAX_OC_LMT       AS "maxOcLmt"
        FROM CORP c
        JOIN COM_SRVC_PC sp
          ON sp.CD = c.SRVC_PC_CD
        JOIN COM_LMT_PC_DTL v
          ON v.LMT_PC_CD = sp.LIMIT_PACKAGE_FK
        <include refid="ccyMatrix"/>
        WHERE c.ID = #{companyId}
          AND c.IS_DELETE = N'N'
          AND scy.SRVC_CD = #{srvcCd}
          AND scm.CCY_MTRX_CD = #{ccyMtrxCd}
        FETCH FIRST 1 ROWS ONLY
    </select>

    <!--
      Copy the package ceiling down into a per-company row - what the legacy procedure
      INJECT_LIMIT_COMPANY does at company setup, done here because for 1,712 of 2,303
      active companies it was never run. Without this row the company has nowhere to record
      a reservation at all: COM_LMT_PC_DTL is a template shared by everyone on the package
      and carries no usage columns, so writing usage there would charge every other company
      on that package for this one's transaction.

      Both usage columns start at 0 rather than NULL: this row is created at the moment of a
      reservation that is about to be added to it, and a NULL would make the increment's
      NVL(...) the only thing standing between the counter and a silent no-op. VERSION 0 is
      the legacy convention for a freshly created row and CREATED_DT is SYSDATE so every
      environment stamps database time.

      LMT_PC_CD is deliberately left NULL. It is nullable on CORP_LMT_PC_DTL and it means
      "the package this ceiling was born from" - useful provenance, but the caller passes
      only the resolved figures, and inventing a package code here from a second lookup
      would let this row claim a lineage the numbers may not actually have.
    -->
    <insert id="insertCorpLimitFromPackage">
        INSERT INTO CORP_LMT_PC_DTL (ID, VERSION, CORP_ID, SRVC_CD, SRVC_CCY_MTRX_ID,
                                     CCY_CD, MAX_AMT_LMT, MAX_OC_LMT,
                                     AMT_LMT_USAGE, OC_LMT_USAGE,
                                     CREATED_BY, CREATED_DT)
        VALUES (#{id}, 0, #{companyId}, #{srvcCd}, #{srvcCcyMtrxId},
                #{ccyCd}, #{maxAmtLmt}, #{maxOcLmt},
                0, 0,
                #{createdBy}, SYSDATE)
    </insert>

    <!--
      Move the company row's two counters. Addressed by ID - the id carried out of
      lockCorpLimit, never a re-resolution, because of the documented duplicates.

      A NEGATIVE amount and count is HOW A RELEASE WORKS. Rejecting a task, or an execution
      that fails, calls this with the negated amount and -1; there is no separate release
      statement, on purpose, because a release using different arithmetic than the
      reservation would drift from it. Nothing is floored at zero and nothing should be: a
      release always matches an earlier reservation on this same row, so a GREATEST(...,0)
      could only ever swallow the evidence of a double release or a release against the
      wrong row. Usage that goes negative is a signal, not a state to be tidied away.

      NVL on both sides because legacy rows created before this application exist with NULL
      counters, and NULL + 1 is NULL - the increment would quietly do nothing.
    -->
    <update id="addCorpUsage">
        UPDATE CORP_LMT_PC_DTL
        SET AMT_LMT_USAGE = NVL(AMT_LMT_USAGE, 0) + #{amount},
            OC_LMT_USAGE  = NVL(OC_LMT_USAGE, 0) + #{count}
        WHERE ID = #{id}
    </update>

    <!--
      The same for the group row, money only. #{count} is accepted by the mapper method so
      both ladders can be driven with one call shape, and is intentionally not referenced
      here: CORP_USR_GRP_LMT has no OC_LMT_USAGE column (see the header). Same negative-is-a-
      release and same no-floor reasoning as addCorpUsage.
    -->
    <update id="addGroupUsage">
        UPDATE CORP_USR_GRP_LMT
        SET AMT_LMT_USAGE = NVL(AMT_LMT_USAGE, 0) + #{amount}
        WHERE ID = #{id}
    </update>

</mapper>
